1.0.0 2026-09-14
    - Initial BIDS conversion from the original per-run CSV exports (see ../sourcedata/).
    - One participant was recorded twice under two source IDs (S03 and S31) due to an
      acquisition error: the first recording (source S03) had the open-loop and
      closed-loop movement cues mislabelled to the non-dominant side. The corrected,
      internally-consistent recording (source S31) was kept and relabelled as sub-03;
      the faulty source-S03 recording was excluded. All other 31 - 1 = 30 participants
      map one-to-one to their original source IDs (sub-XX = source SXX).
    - This dataset (Dataset C) contains only the closed-loop task; see Dataset_A
      (calibration) and Dataset_B (open-loop) for the same participants' other tasks.
    - task-lower_eeg.json / task-upper_eeg.json TaskDescription fields were updated
      with empirically measured cue/rest timing (see Methods of the associated
      publication for the full analysis).

1.1.0 2026-09-16
    - Documentation-only release: no data files changed. Technical validation of the
      released _events.tsv files identified 17 events (0.098% of 17,400 events across
      the three datasets, in 11 of 600 recordings) whose raw TTL 'value' deviates from
      the empirically-dominant code for their (trial_type, preceding trial_type)
      condition, generally co-occurring with an atypical cue duration. Two mechanisms
      were identified by inspection: (1) in Dataset_A, an isolated spurious
      short-duration re-trigger immediately following a genuine movement cue, whose
      code and that of the following rest/movement cue are affected until the next
      true transition resynchronises them; (2) in Dataset_B/C, an occasional missed
      first transition of the run, producing one elongated first-movement-cue event
      that still carries the preceding baseline rest's code. Both are attributed to the
      screen-based event-synchronisation system described in the associated
      publication's Methods, not to the EEG acquisition itself; 'trial_type' (read from
      the acquisition software's own event label, independent of the TRIG channel) is
      unaffected. Values were left untouched, as befits raw TTL data - see
      ../derivatives/validation/flagged_events.tsv for the itemised list (dataset,
      subject, task, run, event index, duration vs. the condition's typical duration)
      and ../code/validation/ for the scripts that produced it.

2.0.0 2026-09-24
    - Amplitude-scale correction: every EEG sample value in every _eeg.eeg file across
      the three sibling datasets was rescaled. The previous release (1.1.0) inherited
      an unchanged assumption from 1.0.0 that the acquisition software's per-run CSV
      exports (see ../sourcedata/) reported scalp-channel amplitude in millivolts, and
      converted to volts for BIDS storage accordingly. Technical validation
      (../code/validation/03_signal_quality_check.py), which computes a per-channel
      standard deviation across the full corpus, showed this produced a median std of
      ~0.05 uV - two to three orders of magnitude below any physiologically plausible
      scalp EEG signal (typically 10-50 uV) - indicating a unit-conversion error rather
      than a genuine recording issue.
    - Re-examination of the raw CSV values (not just their order of magnitude, but the
      resulting per-channel standard deviation and its spatial distribution across the
      scalp) established that the acquisition software already reports amplitude
      directly in volts; no rescaling is needed before BIDS export. Recomputing with
      this corrected assumption gives a corpus-wide median per-channel std of
      48.97 uV, with the expected topography: lowest at central/parietal sites
      (CP1, Pz, CP2, Cz: 24-27 uV) and highest at the frontopolar sites nearest the
      eyes (Fp1, Fp2, Fpz: 101-106 uV, consistent with eye-blink/EOG contamination) -
      both the magnitude and the spatial pattern now match established scalp EEG
      literature, which the previous scaling did not.
    - Only the numeric EEG sample values are affected. Event timing and labels
      (_events.tsv), the TRIG/STATUS auxiliary channels, channel names, sampling rate,
      and all dataset metadata (participants.tsv, channels.tsv,
      dataset_description.json, etc.) are unchanged by this correction.
    - See ../code/csv_to_bids.py for the corrected conversion script and
      ../derivatives/validation/03_signal_quality_summary.csv for the per-recording,
      per-channel signal-quality report computed on the corrected data.

2.1.0 2026-09-30
    - Metadata-only release: no EEG sample values changed. Technical validation
      (../code/validation/07_bad_channels.py) identified scalp channels unusable for
      the whole recording session of three participants (electrodes are placed once
      per ~2 h session, so a genuine electrode problem persists across all of a
      participant's recordings in the three sibling datasets). These channels are now
      marked status=bad, with a status_description, in new recording-level
      sub-XX_task-<task>_run-<run>_channels.tsv files (written by
      ../code/validation/07b_write_bad_channels.py), which take precedence over the
      dataset-wide channels.tsv for those recordings only:
        - sub-11: FC1, FC2, C3 - gel bridge within the group with unstable contact;
        - sub-12: F3, Fz, F4, FC1, FC2 - gel bridge within the group with unstable
          contact;
        - sub-14: Fz - high noise / poor contact.
      Criteria: a gel bridge is a group of channels whose 30-45 Hz band-passed signals
      correlate at r >= 0.985 in at least 75% of the participant's recordings and whose
      0.5 Hz high-passed amplitude is at least 4x the corpus-wide median for those
      channels; a noisy channel has a 1-45 Hz standard deviation at least 3x the median
      of its four nearest neighbours in at least 75% of the participant's recordings.
      Channels were not interpolated or removed: users may exclude or interpolate them
      as suited to their analysis. See ../derivatives/validation/
      07_bad_channels_proposal.csv and 07_bad_channels_per_recording.csv, and the
      "Known data-quality issues" section of the README for borderline cases that were
      deliberately not marked bad.

2.2.0 2026-09-30
    - Metadata-only release: no EEG sample values changed.
    - channels.tsv (dataset-wide and the recording-level files introduced in 2.1.0)
      gains a 'description' column. It documents that TRIG holds the raw TTL code
      of the event-synchronisation system and that STATUS is the amplifier's
      running sample counter, which continues across runs until the amplifier is
      restarted and can therefore be used to recover the acquisition order and
      the pauses between runs (../code/validation/09_timing_and_session.py,
      ../derivatives/validation/09_session_runs.csv).
    - README: new section "Acquisition order and session timeline", noting that
      run numbers follow the source-file numbering and differ from the
      acquisition order in sub-06, sub-24 and sub-26.

2.2.1 2026-09-30
    - Metadata-only release: no EEG sample values changed. Fixes from the official
      BIDS validator (bids-validator 3.0.2), which now reports no errors:
        - dataset_description.json: removed "Authors" (error: mutually exclusive
          with CITATION.cff) and "License", "HowToAcknowledge" and
          "ReferencesAndLinks" (single-source citation fields); authors, license
          and citation message remain in CITATION.cff, to which the
          ClinicalTrials.gov registration link was added under "identifiers".
        - task-*_eeg.json: "MiscChannelCount" renamed to the correct key
          "MISCChannelCount"; added "InstitutionalDepartmentName".

2.2.2 2026-09-30
    - TRIG and STATUS are now stored at resolution 1 (previously 0.1) in every
      _eeg.vhdr, so the amplifier's STATUS sample counter is an exact integer in
      all recordings. Previously, 6 recordings (sub-09, task-upper, runs 01-03 of
      Dataset_B and Dataset_C; none in Dataset_A) held STATUS values off by a
      fraction of a sample due to float32 precision at 0.1 resolution; their
      STATUS values are now exact. EEG and TRIG values are unchanged in all 600
      recordings of the three datasets (verified by reloading every recording
      before and after the change).
    - README and channels.tsv (dataset-wide and recording-level): removed the
      now-unneeded instruction to round STATUS to the nearest integer.
    - All files are now produced by ../code/csv_to_bids.py followed by
      ../code/validation/07_bad_channels.py and 07b_write_bad_channels.py.

2.3.0 2026-10-01
    - Metadata-only release: no EEG, TRIG or STATUS values changed. Adds fields
      recommended by the BIDS specification:
        - dataset_description.json: "Funding" (UNER PID-UNER 6235; ANPCyT PICT
          2017-4596) and "SourceDatasets" (../sourcedata, the original CSV exports).
        - task-*_eeg.json: "SoftwareVersions" (OpenViBE 3.6.0 Acquisition Server with
          the ANT Neuro eego driver, together with custom Python 3.12 acquisition
          software), "DeviceSerialNumber" and "SubjectArtefactDescription"
          ("n/a": no major artefact source other than blinks and cardiac activity).
        - events.json: "StimulusPresentation" (custom Python software developed
          in-house).
        - One _eeg.json sidecar per recording (600 new files) holding its
          "RecordingDuration" in seconds; all other fields are inherited from
          task-*_eeg.json.

2.3.1 2026-10-07
    - Documentation-only release: no data files changed. README: the participants'
      age was corrected from "28 +/- 6 years" to "28.8 +/- 7.0 years, range 21-43",
      which is the mean, standard deviation and range of the 'age' column of
      participants.tsv (unchanged).

2.3.2 2026-10-11
    - Validation-only release: no data or metadata files changed. Adds
      ../code/validation/11_classification_controls.py and its outputs
      (../derivatives/validation/11_classification_controls_results.csv and
      11_classification_controls_summary.csv): controls for the decoding
      benchmark of 10_classification_benchmark.py (later analysis window,
      occipital channels only, ~18 Hz line not removed, and Dataset_A
      subsampled to the trial count of Dataset_B and Dataset_C).

2.4.0 2026-10-14
    - Signal files unchanged. _events.tsv: a 'feedback' row (sham-feedback mirror
      animation; onset = onset of the rest cue that follows each movement cue,
      duration = programmed 5 s lower limb / 3 s upper limb, value n/a) was added
      after each such rest row (10 per run, 1,800 in total); all existing rows are
      unchanged. events.json defines the new level and the duration column.
    - Licence changed from CC0 1.0
      to CC BY 4.0, the licence of the earlier release of these recordings
      (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17980608, FIF format, December 2025), which
      is now declared in README and CITATION.cff. This BIDS release is published
      as a new version of that Zenodo deposit.
    - Evoked-potential validation (../code/validation/04*): in Dataset_B and
      Dataset_C the auditory evoked potential is now averaged over the movement
      cues only, the only cues that carry the beep.

2.5.0 2026-10-20
    - Metadata-only release: no EEG or event files changed. task-*_eeg.json and
      README now give the amplifier model (eego EE-212, 24-bit, DC-coupled) and the
      cap (waveguard, passive Ag/AgCl electrodes).
    - Dataset_A: the movement cues (arrows) were accompanied by the same 1,000 Hz,
      100 ms beep used in Datasets B and C, as programmed in the stimulus script
      (../code/stimulus_presentation/Task_1.py) and confirmed by an auditory N1-P2
      response to movement but not rest cues (../code/validation/04d_*). The task
      description previously described these cues as visual only.
    - Stimuli added: stimuli/ in Dataset_A (cue images) and Dataset_C (mirror-
      animation frames); stimulus-presentation scripts in ../code/stimulus_presentation/.
    - Validation scripts added (../code/validation/): 00 (CSV vs BrainVision
      round trip), 04d (auditory response in Dataset_A), 05d (beta rebound latency),
      07c (gel bridges by electrical distance), 12 (horizontal eye movements, F7-F8);
      05b now draws the measured median cue offset of each dataset.
